"Artemis the Ranger" <wcristalle@hotmail.com> wrote:
> But what it seems Gehennom needs is some way in which it is
> drastically different from the rest of the game -- in the scenery, at
> least, or perhaps even the fundamental rules of reality. This is
> *Hell*, after all! You should know, very clearly, that you're no
> longer in the land of the living.

Agreed.  But pretty much all of the ideas I've seen would make it
_impossible_ for relatively new (or unspoiled) players to survive.

> So after some thought, I came up with six possibilities. Hell could
> be:
>
> 1) Mythos-specific
> [SNIP]
> Advantages: makes ascensions of different classes much more
> interesting.
> Disadvantages: still would get monotonous after a while, if you play
> enough characters of the same class. Spoilable.

Not bad--players would still be able to work their way through, without
suddenly feeling like they're being forced to learn a completely new game.
I don't think it's really that big of a deal that it's "spoilable"--if
you're reading spoilers, can you really complain about being spoiled?

> 2) Your worst nightmare
> [SNIP]
> Advantages: would be highly character-specific; one would never
> encounter the same Gehennom twice; gives players a real challenge by
> finding their weaknesses; non-spoilable to some degree
> Disadvantages: perhaps too difficult and frustrating; to some degree
> increases the "scavenger hunt" nature of the game because a player
> will want to ensure they have all the resistances (though this could
> be offset by stepping up gremlin attacks)

Interesting, and has potential, though the logic could get really hairy, and
most people have most intrinsics by the time they reach Gehennom anyhow.
And, really, most players' "worst nightmares" would be disenchanters,
arch-liches, archons, and master mind flayers on a no-teleport level.  Or
maybe that's just me.  But, regardless, this would be really hard to balance
(could the player trick the game into thinking that grid bugs and newts were
their worst nightmares by running away from all of them?), and thus probably
isn't a good idea.

> 3) The home of dead creatures
> [SNIP]
> Advantages: reduces the overuse of genocide scrolls, causes you to
> think about whether you really want to kill something or just evade
> it, reducing "hack your way through the dungeon" style fighting
> Disadvantages: nothing really new, doesn't change the scenery of
> Gehennom, spoilable

Sheesh, let's just make things difficult for newbies, and easy for
experienced players, shall we?  Newbies, having killed pretty much
everything they come across, and genocided certain critters they've had
trouble with, suddenly find Gehennom packed with all sorts of monsters,
especially L and [master] mind flayers!  Lovely!  Meanwhile, experienced
(spoiled) players genocide newts and grid bugs, and maybe c*ckatrices, in
order to have a fairly easy walk through the lower levels...not to mention
what happens to extinctionist characters.  Too many problems here.

> 4) A place where nothing stays dead
> [SNIP]
> Advantages: speeds up trip through Gehennom, makes for more
> interesting fighting, poses a challenge to even well-armoured fighters
> Disadvantages: promotes melee fighting over other methods of killing
> (as your wands, scrolls, arrows, potions etc. will get used up, but
> your sword won't.) This could make life difficult for Tourists,
> Wizards, Rangers, etc.

Again, absolutely horrid for relatively new characters.  Most newbies have
trouble with trolls--how many actually think to eat/tin a troll to keep it
down, rather than just sighing and hitting it again when they stand up?  Of
course, this also makes c*ckatrice corpses even more useful than they
already are...

> 5) The Land of Chaos
> [SNIP]
> Advantages: makes for a much more difficult and exciting game; reduces
> ability of players to mindlessly plow through; requires you go on
> looking for new objects once in Gehennom (to replace those you've
> lost)
> Disadvantages: may increase YAADs (due to inconvenient changing of
> your equipment at crucial moments); some may find it annoying and
> infuriating.

This is, quite frankly, probably the WORST idea possible.  Not only does it
make Gehennom impossible for relatively new players, it does so even for
very experienced ones!  (Example: You enter Gehennom.  Your gloves change
shape!  Your gloves become a cockatrice corpse!  You turn to stone! or: Your
amulet of life saving changes shape!  It constricts your throat!)  There's
no "may" about this: it *will* increase YAADs (due to inconvenient changing
of your equipment *ever*)and everyone *will* find it annoying and
infuriating.  Except for maybe masochists.

(Incidentally, this criticism also holds for the equally silly idea of
having all items suddenly have different effects.  Your luckstone becomes a
load stone; unicorn horn becomes a tooled horn; scroll of identify becomes a
scroll of amnesia; boots of speed become boots of fumbling; helm of
brilliance becomes a dunce cap; amulet of life saving becomes an amulet of
change and disappers; etc., etc., ad naseum.  This would turn so many
players, new and experienced, off of NH so quickly that you'd be able to
hear the dungeons collapsing.)

> 6) Land of puzzles/riddles
> [SNIP]
> Advantages: new, never seen before; unspoilable; adds a different
> dimension to the game
> Disadvantages: some people may find it frustrating.

Again, I'm not sure of the need for "unspoilable".  And, of course, there's
the issue of what you mean by that.  For instance, I would never have
thought on my own to bring a musical instrument to the castle, until my god
gave me the tune; but because I was spoiled (yes, I was, and feel no shame
about it), and knew that the puzzle was there, I grabbed the first (and
only) instrument I found to make sure I was ready.  Most puzzles/riddles are
"spoilable" just by warning the player that they're even potentially there.

That being said, I'm not against this idea.  It wouldn't make the game
impossible for newbies, and, much like the Heck^2 patch, would make Gehennom
more interesting even for experienced players.

So, to sum up: ideas 1 and 6 are feasible, and potentially quite interesting
and fun; idea 2 is interesting, though very difficult to implement/balance;
ideas 3, 4, and 5 should be taken out and shot immediately, if not sooner.
:-)

